1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,554
Two magnets snap together
and stop at one exact gap.

2
00:00:03,554 --> 00:00:06,055
A folding chair opens to one fixed angle.

3
00:00:06,055 --> 00:00:08,520
A rope is harder to snap than a thread.

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:10,369
Gap, angle, strength.

5
00:00:10,369 --> 00:00:12,362
Every chemical bond has these too.

6
00:00:12,812 --> 00:00:15,082
Aarav straps his bag to his bicycle.

7
00:00:15,082 --> 00:00:16,921
With one strap, it wobbles.

8
00:00:16,921 --> 00:00:19,660
With three straps, it
sits tight and close.

9
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:21,868
Do atoms work the same way?

10
00:00:21,868 --> 00:00:24,645
Do more bonds hold them
closer and stronger?

11
00:00:25,145 --> 00:00:28,229
By the end, you will measure
a bond like an engineer.

12
00:00:28,879 --> 00:00:32,773
Chemists describe a bond with four
numbers, called bond parameters.

13
00:00:33,123 --> 00:00:34,023
One.

14
00:00:33,632 --> 00:00:34,599
Bond length.

15
00:00:34,599 --> 00:00:38,958
The distance between the centres of
two bonded atoms, like two friends

16
00:00:38,958 --> 00:00:41,711
holding hands, measured middle to middle.

17
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:42,961
Two.

18
00:00:42,547 --> 00:00:43,447
Bond angle.

19
00:00:43,423 --> 00:00:47,744
The angle between two bonds around
the middle atom, like the angle of a

20
00:00:47,744 --> 00:00:48,763
folding chair.

21
00:00:49,113 --> 00:00:50,013
Three.

22
00:00:49,724 --> 00:00:50,797
Bond enthalpy.

23
00:00:50,797 --> 00:00:55,097
The energy to break one mole of
a bond, with the atoms as gas.

24
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:57,373
Like the pull that snaps a rope.

25
00:00:57,723 --> 00:00:58,623
Four.

26
00:00:58,247 --> 00:00:59,147
Bond order.

27
00:00:59,108 --> 00:01:01,514
The number of bonds between two atoms.

28
00:01:01,514 --> 00:01:03,770
One strap, two straps, or three.

29
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:06,643
Let's measure on our molecule bench.

30
00:01:06,643 --> 00:01:09,451
Two oxygen atoms share
one pair of electrons.

31
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:10,516
A single bond.

32
00:01:10,866 --> 00:01:13,472
The ruler reads one hundred
forty eight picometres.

33
00:01:13,472 --> 00:01:15,927
A picometre is a million
millionth of a metre.

34
00:01:16,327 --> 00:01:18,256
Now they share a second pair.

35
00:01:18,256 --> 00:01:19,314
A double bond.

36
00:01:19,314 --> 00:01:21,140
Watch the atoms move closer.

37
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:23,331
One hundred twenty one picometres.

38
00:01:23,331 --> 00:01:25,949
Two pairs pull harder,
so the bond gets shorter.

39
00:01:26,349 --> 00:01:27,497
Now, how strong?

40
00:01:27,497 --> 00:01:29,802
Hydrogen, H two, has bond order one.

41
00:01:29,802 --> 00:01:34,056
Breaking one mole of it takes four hundred
thirty five point eight kilojoules.

42
00:01:34,456 --> 00:01:38,076
Oxygen, bond order two, takes
four hundred ninety eight.

43
00:01:38,076 --> 00:01:41,743
Nitrogen, bond order three,
takes nine hundred forty six.

44
00:01:42,143 --> 00:01:47,415
So as bond order goes up, bond enthalpy
goes up and bond length goes down.

45
00:01:48,065 --> 00:01:50,957
Compare the two oxygen bonds side by side.

46
00:01:50,957 --> 00:01:52,848
One shared pair, or two.

47
00:01:53,198 --> 00:01:54,430
Pause and think.

48
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:56,233
Which is harder to break?

49
00:01:56,233 --> 00:01:59,448
The OO single bond, or the OO double bond?

50
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:03,673
The double bond!

51
00:02:03,673 --> 00:02:06,457
Two shared pairs hold the
atoms tighter and closer.

52
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:09,686
Now, where does a bond length come from?

53
00:02:09,686 --> 00:02:10,671
Step by step.

54
00:02:11,021 --> 00:02:11,921
Step one.

55
00:02:11,757 --> 00:02:15,309
In H two, the two nuclei are
seventy four picometres apart.

56
00:02:16,059 --> 00:02:16,959
Step two.

57
00:02:16,806 --> 00:02:19,573
Both atoms are the
same, so each gets half.

58
00:02:19,573 --> 00:02:21,057
Thirty seven picometres.

59
00:02:21,057 --> 00:02:23,487
This is the covalent radius of hydrogen.

60
00:02:24,237 --> 00:02:25,137
Step three.

61
00:02:25,097 --> 00:02:29,349
For a bond between A and B, the
length is about the covalent radius of

62
00:02:29,349 --> 00:02:30,657
A plus that of B.

63
00:02:31,407 --> 00:02:32,307
Step four.

64
00:02:32,189 --> 00:02:36,170
Atoms of two molecules that touch
but do not bond sit farther apart.

65
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:38,767
Half that gap is the van der Waals radius.

66
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:39,737
It is bigger.

67
00:02:40,487 --> 00:02:42,274
Bond angle tells us shape.

68
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:46,959
In water, the two OH bonds meet at
one hundred four point five degrees.

69
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:48,050
Water is bent.

70
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:52,531
Molecules with the same number of
electrons are called isoelectronic.

71
00:02:52,531 --> 00:02:54,411
They have the same bond order.

72
00:02:54,411 --> 00:02:57,645
N two, CO and NO plus all
have bond order three.

73
00:02:58,295 --> 00:02:59,377
Remember this.

74
00:02:59,377 --> 00:03:02,694
Higher bond order means
a shorter, stronger bond.

75
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:05,084
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:05,384 --> 00:03:07,132
Water has two OH bonds.

77
00:03:07,132 --> 00:03:10,948
Breaking the first needs five
hundred two kilojoules per mole.

78
00:03:10,948 --> 00:03:13,972
The second needs only
four hundred twenty seven.

79
00:03:14,472 --> 00:03:15,457
Why different?

80
00:03:15,457 --> 00:03:19,481
After one hydrogen leaves, the
remaining bond is in a new setting.

81
00:03:19,981 --> 00:03:21,583
So we take the average.

82
00:03:21,583 --> 00:03:25,606
Five hundred two plus four hundred
twenty seven is nine hundred twenty

83
00:03:25,606 --> 00:03:26,506
nine.

84
00:03:26,884 --> 00:03:27,960
Divide by two.

85
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:31,331
Four hundred sixty four point
five kilojoules per mole.

86
00:03:31,331 --> 00:03:34,378
That is the average OH
bond enthalpy in water.

87
00:03:35,028 --> 00:03:36,490
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:40,772
Some students think more bonds make
a longer bond, because there is more

89
00:03:40,772 --> 00:03:41,672
in between.

90
00:03:41,971 --> 00:03:43,688
It is the other way round.

91
00:03:43,688 --> 00:03:46,088
More shared pairs pull the atoms closer.

92
00:03:46,088 --> 00:03:48,947
OO single, one hundred
forty eight picometres.

93
00:03:48,947 --> 00:03:51,213
OO double, one hundred twenty one.

94
00:03:51,863 --> 00:03:53,255
Where is this asked?

95
00:03:53,255 --> 00:03:57,479
Arrange bonds by length or strength,
and short numericals on average bond

96
00:03:57,479 --> 00:03:58,379
enthalpy.

97
00:03:58,789 --> 00:04:00,048
Exam level now.

98
00:04:00,048 --> 00:04:04,786
The average OH bond enthalpy in water
is four hundred sixty four point

99
00:04:04,786 --> 00:04:06,643
five kilojoules per mole.

100
00:04:06,643 --> 00:04:10,943
How much energy breaks every
OH bond in two moles of water?

101
00:04:10,943 --> 00:04:12,256
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:17,656 --> 00:04:18,777
The answer is B.

103
00:04:18,777 --> 00:04:21,533
One thousand eight hundred
fifty eight kilojoules.

104
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:24,413
Each water has two OH bonds.

105
00:04:24,413 --> 00:04:28,365
So two moles of water hold
four moles of OH bonds.

106
00:04:28,865 --> 00:04:33,217
Four times four hundred sixty four
point five is one thousand eight hundred

107
00:04:33,217 --> 00:04:34,139
fifty eight.

108
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:36,992
A counts one mole of water only.

109
00:04:36,992 --> 00:04:41,748
C uses five hundred two for every
bond, but the bonds are not equal.

110
00:04:42,348 --> 00:04:43,889
Here is your recap.

111
00:04:43,889 --> 00:04:45,472
Screenshot this card.

112
00:04:48,672 --> 00:04:50,857
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:04:51,257 --> 00:04:52,157
One.

114
00:04:51,775 --> 00:04:55,095
What is the bond order
of N two, and of CO?

115
00:04:55,895 --> 00:04:56,795
Two.

116
00:04:56,343 --> 00:04:59,647
Why is the OO double bond
shorter than the single bond?

117
00:05:00,447 --> 00:05:01,347
Three.

118
00:05:00,998 --> 00:05:05,140
If H two is seventy four picometres
long, what is the covalent radius of

119
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:06,040
hydrogen?

120
00:05:06,726 --> 00:05:10,845
Next time: why are both bonds in
ozone exactly the same length?

121
00:05:10,845 --> 00:05:13,197
We find out in resonance structures.
